al strategy because, I give my teammate control, not the Landlord.\n\n**Decision & Implementation:** I need to set my teammate up. Playing the 6 is the clear answer here. Even if the Landlord has high cards, playing the 6 gives my teammate maximum flexibility.\n\n**The "Why" Behind the "What":** The critical factor is my teammate\'s turn. I can\'t let the Landlord retain control or play any high cards to dominate. My hand is so weak, but this strategy has the best odds, especially with an unknown factor in my hand. It is simple but effective, and, given this board state, my best strategic move is ♣6.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold four single cards: 6, 10, Q, K. My teammate (Next Player) has only 2 cards remaining, while the Landlord (Previous Player) has 5 cards. The count of unknown cards indicates no pairs are possible in the opponents\' hands (all ranks have count 1). Since my teammate plays immediately after me, my goal is to "feed" them a card they can easily beat or use to take control.\n\nReason: Playing the lowest single (♣6) is the optimal cooperative move. It allows my teammate to play a wide range of cards. If they hold a medium card (like an 8 or 10), they can beat the 6 and reduce their hand size. If they hold a high card (A or 2), they can take the lead. Playing a high card like the King would likely force my teammate to pass or waste a top card unnecessarily.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣6"]}'}
